&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Query works best when server-state management follows a clear and consistent architecture. Shared query keys, reusable API functions, intentional cache settings, and mutation-driven invalidation make applications easier to maintain while reducing unnecessary network requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with a shared QueryClient and a small set of custom query hooks, then introduce more advanced techniques such as optimistic updates, pagination, and dependent queries as your application grows. Avoid adding configuration simply because it exists; every cache and refetch option should serve a specific requirement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most importantly, treat loading states, errors, and cache consistency as core parts of the user experience rather than implementation details. When these practices are applied consistently, TanStack Query becomes a dependable foundation for building responsive, scalable React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

